    {
      "id": "sHbkBDpanEwF62oFttFJt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -3382.077816836033,
      "y": 124.06387957631955,
      "width": 1174.359375,
      "height": 2525,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1710430223,
      "version": 4246,
      "versionNonce": 1492915780,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785809199525,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Scale problems\n\n1.1 Handle traffic spikes\n1.2 Handle underlying API failures\n\n2. High Level Architecture\n\nStateful Service\n\n2.1 Async Pre generation of forecasts\n2.2 Forecasts Cache\n\n3. System Design\n\n3.1 Producer/Consumer\n\nGenerator <<Producer>>\n    Worker Pool (Fetchers)\n\n    Saves into:\n        Forecast Buffer (channel)\n        Forecast Cache\n\nEndpoint handler <<Consumer>>\n\n\n4. Trade Offs\n\n- Additional memory usage\n- Risk of stale data\n\n5. Limitations\n\n- Static worker pool size. It doesnt grow as demand grows.\n- Cache is good for a reduced time period. We are resilient to 3rd party failures for as long as the cache is valid.\n    - Business question? What is the impact of serving expired data vs the impact of not serving data?\n\n\nWhy is there a cache and a channel?\n\n- The channel is used to synchronize the workers, while the \ncache is used to serve the consumers when the channel (buffered) is\ndepleted.\n\nHow would you implement this using sync primitives other than\nchannel?\n\nRW Mutex\n    Writer Locks/Unlocks\n          BufferSizeChannel <- // This blocks once the buffer is full, just a token to keep track of the size of the cache\n\n    Reader locks/unlocks\n           <- BufferSizeChannel // This unblocks a go routine to replenish the buffer\n\nInternal flows of each component:\n\nHandler\n    Context with timeout\n        Block on context timeout\n                    Read from cache\n        Block on channel response\n                    Read from channel response \n\n\nGenerator\n    wg.Add\n    go ( loop(){channel <- fetcher.fetch}; wg.done())\n\n    wg.Wait()\n    close(channel)\n\n\nFetcher\n\n    RequestPerSecTarget = 50\n    TimeBetweenRequest = 1000ms/50 request = 20ms\n    Limiter = 20 ms\n\n    \n    Context with timeout\n\n        Block on context timeout\n        Block on <-time.After(limiter)\n              Fetcher.Fetch\n\n        Block on write locations<-forecast\n        Block on timeout\n\nHandler\n\n    Context with timeout\n        Loop{\n            Block on context timeout\n                readFromCache\n                return body\n            Block on locationChan read\n                if read.expiration < time.now continue\n                else return body\n        }\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Scale problems\n\n1.1 Handle traffic spikes\n1.2 Handle underlying API failures\n\n2. High Level Architecture\n\nStateful Service\n\n2.1 Async Pre generation of forecasts\n2.2 Forecasts Cache\n\n3. System Design\n\n3.1 Producer/Consumer\n\nGenerator <<Producer>>\n    Worker Pool (Fetchers)\n\n    Saves into:\n        Forecast Buffer (channel)\n        Forecast Cache\n\nEndpoint handler <<Consumer>>\n\n\n4. Trade Offs\n\n- Additional memory usage\n- Risk of stale data\n\n5. Limitations\n\n- Static worker pool size. It doesnt grow as demand grows.\n- Cache is good for a reduced time period. We are resilient to 3rd party failures for as long as the cache is valid.\n    - Business question? What is the impact of serving expired data vs the impact of not serving data?\n\n\nWhy is there a cache and a channel?\n\n- The channel is used to synchronize the workers, while the \ncache is used to serve the consumers when the channel (buffered) is\ndepleted.\n\nHow would you implement this using sync primitives other than\nchannel?\n\nRW Mutex\n    Writer Locks/Unlocks\n          BufferSizeChannel <- // This blocks once the buffer is full, just a token to keep track of the size of the cache\n\n    Reader locks/unlocks\n           <- BufferSizeChannel // This unblocks a go routine to replenish the buffer\n\nInternal flows of each component:\n\nHandler\n    Context with timeout\n        Block on context timeout\n                    Read from cache\n        Block on channel response\n                    Read from channel response \n\n\nGenerator\n    wg.Add\n    go ( loop(){channel <- fetcher.fetch}; wg.done())\n\n    wg.Wait()\n    close(channel)\n\n\nFetcher\n\n    RequestPerSecTarget = 50\n    TimeBetweenRequest = 1000ms/50 request = 20ms\n    Limiter = 20 ms\n\n    \n    Context with timeout\n\n        Block on context timeout\n        Block on <-time.After(limiter)\n              Fetcher.Fetch\n\n        Block on write locations<-forecast\n        Block on timeout\n\nHandler\n\n    Context with timeout\n        Loop{\n            Block on context timeout\n                readFromCache\n                return body\n            Block on locationChan read\n                if read.expiration < time.now continue\n                else return body\n        }\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
