        }
      ],
      "updated": 1785812478880,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Horizontal Design Changes\n\nProblems to solve\n\n1. Traffic Spikes\n2. Resiliency to 3rd party API failures/throughtput\n\nArchitecture\n\n- AutoScaler/Load Balancer\n- Web Server (Front End Role)\n- Forecast Generator (Producer Role)\n- Distributed cache \n\nSystem Design\n\nWeb Server\n    Stateless service, scales horizontally\n    Expose health and response metrics\n\nAuto Scaler\n    Reads Web Server Metrics and deploys new instances dynamically\n\nLoad Balancer\n    Distributes requests across front end instances, relies on auto scaler\ndata, liveness and ready probes.\n\nDistributed Cache\n    Holds the location information, its used to evict old records either by\nkeeping a TTL per item or a collection sorted by expiration time.\n\nGenerator Role\n    Responsible of replenishing the cache, it may use a push or pull model, by\nusing Redis notifications we can refresh the cache as soon as it expires\n    since this notification is a fire and forget, we may use a MQ in the\nmiddle to a durable track of this work. Another option is to continuously\npoll for the size of the \n    collection after all the elements expire and replenish if needed.\n\n    Pro of push model: We are notified immediately if the cache size is below\nthe required threshold. \n    Cons of push model: Relies on simple fire and forget notifications,\nrequire an MQ for persistence, additional infrastructure and complexity.\n\n    Pro of pull model: No need for additional infrastructure, state itself is\nthe collection on the distributed cache.\n    Cons: Delayed response to replenish the cache, as big as the pull loop\ndelay.\n\n\n    Cache Monitor\n        Responsible for continously polling the cache state and fire new jobs.\n        \n    Fetcher\n        Responsible for the sequential call to the third party service\n    \n    Random Location cache\n        Fallback cache to generate random locations.",
      "fontSize": 18.727678571428573,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Horizontal Design Changes\n\nProblems to solve\n\n1. Traffic Spikes\n2. Resiliency to 3rd party API failures/throughtput\n\nArchitecture\n\n- AutoScaler/Load Balancer\n- Web Server (Front End Role)\n- Forecast Generator (Producer Role)\n- Distributed cache \n\nSystem Design\n\nWeb Server\n    Stateless service, scales horizontally\n    Expose health and response metrics\n\nAuto Scaler\n    Reads Web Server Metrics and deploys new instances dynamically\n\nLoad Balancer\n    Distributes requests across front end instances, relies on auto scaler data, liveness and ready probes.\n\nDistributed Cache\n    Holds the location information, its used to evict old records either by keeping a TTL per item or a collection sorted by expiration time.\n\nGenerator Role\n    Responsible of replenishing the cache, it may use a push or pull model, by using Redis notifications we can refresh the cache as soon as it expires\n    since this notification is a fire and forget, we may use a MQ in the middle to a durable track of this work. Another option is to continuously poll for the size of the \n    collection after all the elements expire and replenish if needed.\n\n    Pro of push model: We are notified immediately if the cache size is below the required threshold. \n    Cons of push model: Relies on simple fire and forget notifications, require an MQ for persistence, additional infrastructure and complexity.\n\n    Pro of pull model: No need for additional infrastructure, state itself is the collection on the distributed cache.\n    Cons: Delayed response to replenish the cache, as big as the pull loop delay.\n\n\n    Cache Monitor\n        Responsible for continously polling the cache state and fire new jobs.\n        \n    Fetcher\n        Responsible for the sequential call to the third party service\n    \n    Random Location cache\n        Fallback cache to generate random locations.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
